WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.300
Hierarchical clustering.

2
00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:06.367
Instead of guessing how many clusters, build every clustering at once.

3
00:00:07.567 --> 00:00:11.300
Hierarchical clustering produces a tree called a dendrogram.

4
00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:15.033
The height of each branch records how different the merged groups were.

5
00:00:15.333 --> 00:00:17.900
Cut the tree at any height, and you get a clustering.

6
00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:20.767
Here is how the tree gets built.

7
00:00:21.067 --> 00:00:23.367
Start with every point as its own cluster.

8
00:00:23.667 --> 00:00:25.567
Merge the two closest clusters.

9
00:00:25.867 --> 00:00:28.314
Repeat until only one cluster remains.

10
00:00:28.314 --> 00:00:30.633
The merge history is the dendrogram.

11
00:00:31.833 --> 00:00:33.700
Now the payoff: cutting the tree.

12
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.067
At the top, everything is one group.

13
00:00:36.367 --> 00:00:38.667
Cut high, and you get two big branches.

14
00:00:38.967 --> 00:00:42.486
Cut lower, and propulsion splits into finer topics.

15
00:00:42.486 --> 00:00:43.867
Same tree, no refit.

16
00:00:45.067 --> 00:00:49.833
One main knob is linkage, which decides how to measure distance between clusters.

17
00:00:50.133 --> 00:00:53.267
Ward minimizes variance, and is the usual default.

18
00:00:53.567 --> 00:00:56.633
Single linkage looks at nearest members, so it chains.

19
00:00:56.933 --> 00:01:00.267
Complete linkage looks at farthest members, so it compacts.

20
00:01:01.467 --> 00:01:02.800
In code, it is short.

21
00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:06.233
We build the full merge tree once, using ward linkage.

22
00:01:06.533 --> 00:01:10.200
Then we cut it into two clusters, or six, without fitting again.

23
00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:12.733
Here is a real use.

24
00:01:13.033 --> 00:01:16.133
You cluster embeddings of two thousand mission log chunks.

25
00:01:16.433 --> 00:01:23.433
Cut high for propulsion versus avionics, or lower for fuel systems, thruster burns, and pressure anomalies.

26
00:01:23.733 --> 00:01:28.667
The dendrogram becomes your browsing taxonomy, and you never guessed the number of topics.

27
00:01:29.867 --> 00:01:30.627
Let's recap.

28
00:01:30.627 --> 00:01:33.033
Keep merging the closest two clusters.

29
00:01:33.333 --> 00:01:35.700
The dendrogram shows structure at every scale.

30
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.295
Cut at any height, no refit needed.

31
00:01:38.295 --> 00:01:40.000
Ward is the usual linkage.

32
00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:41.833
Next up: PCA.
